The failure is at line 4 of the selected function:

`SELECT salary INTO l_salary FROM employees WHERE last_name = p_last_name;`

The context shows that `HARNESS_APP.EMPLOYEES.LAST_NAME` is not unique. A `SELECT ... INTO` must return exactly one row. When more than one employee has the same last name, Oracle raises `ORA-01422`. The user report that it fails for `SMITH` is consistent with there being multiple rows where `last_name = 'SMITH'`, although the context does not show how many such rows exist.

Because the supplied parameter is only `p_last_name`, the function cannot uniquely identify an employee from that value alone. The safe repair is to handle `TOO_MANY_ROWS` explicitly and raise a named application error naming the ambiguous key. This does not choose an arbitrary row, which would hide the ambiguity. I have not compiled or run this unit.

